To find the \(3 \times 3\) matrix \(\mathbf{M}\) such that \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{v} = -4\mathbf{v}\) for all vectors \(\mathbf{v}\) in \(\mathbb{R}^3\), we recognize that this condition means \(\mathbf{M}\) must act as a scalar multiple of the identity matrix. Specifically, multiplying any vector \(\mathbf{v}\) by \(\mathbf{M}\) gives \(-4\mathbf{v}\), which is the same as multiplying \(\mathbf{v}\) by the scalar \(-4\). 

Thus, \(\mathbf{M}\) must be the scalar \(-4\) times the identity matrix of size \(3 \times 3\):

\[
\mathbf{M} = -4 \mathbf{I}_3 = 
\begin{bmatrix}
-4 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -4 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & -4
\end{bmatrix}
\]

To verify, if \(\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}\), then
\[
\mathbf{M}\mathbf{v} = 
\begin{bmatrix}
-4 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -4 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & -4
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-4x \\ -4y \\ -4z
\end{bmatrix}
= -4 \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
= -4 \mathbf{v}.
\]

This confirms the solution is correct.

\[
\boxed{\begin{bmatrix} -4 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -4 \end{bmatrix}}
\]

